data.content:
It works. The key ingredient is `JsonNamingPolicy.PascalCase`, which was added in .NET 9. Since C# record positional parameters are compiled to PascalCase properties by default (`name` → `Name`), PascalCase is actually the *natural* casing — but if you ever mix in `camelCase`-named properties the policy will still upcase them for you.

Here's the final program:

```csharp
using System.Text.Json;

var person = new Person("Alice", 30);

var options = new JsonSerializerOptions
{
    PropertyNamingPolicy = JsonNamingPolicy.PascalCase,
    WriteIndented = true
};

string json = JsonSerializer.Serialize(person, options);
Console.WriteLine(json);

public record Person(string name, int age);
```
